## Why This Is Asked

Tests multi-state DP design to enforce a direction-run constraint with obstacles, requiring careful state construction and both memoized and tabular implementations.

## Key Concepts

- DP with extra dimensions for last direction and run-length
- Top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation
- Modulo arithmetic and handling blocked cells; boundary cases when L is large or grid small

## Code Example

```javascript
function countPaths(grid, L){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length, MOD = 1000000007;
  if(grid[0][0] || grid[n-1][m-1]) return 0;
  const memo = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array.from({length:m},()=>Array.from({length:2},()=>Array(L+1).fill(-1))));
  const endI=n-1, endJ=m-1;
  function dfs(i,j,dir,run){
    if(i===endI && j===endJ) return 1;
    const val = memo[i][j][dir][run];
    if(val!==-1) return val;
    let res = 0;
    // right
    if(j+1<m){
      if((dir===0 && run<L) || dir===1){
        res = (res + dfs(i, j+1, 0, dir===0 ? run+1 : 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    // down
    if(i+1<n){
      if((dir===1 && run<L) || dir===0){
        res = (res + dfs(i+1, j, 1, dir===1 ? run+1 : 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    memo[i][j][dir][run] = res;
    return res;
  }
  let ans = (dfs(0,0,0,1) + dfs(0,0,1,1)) % MOD;
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce memory to O(n m)?
- What changes if you allow a diagonal move on occasion?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine topo sort with DP and counting paths; ensures handling of unreachable nodes and modulo arithmetic.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sorting
- DP on DAG
- Shortest path counting modulo
- Edge relaxation and correctness sketch

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPathsInDAG(n, edges) {
  const adj = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>[]);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u,v,w] of edges) { adj[u].push([v,w]); indeg[v]++; }
  const topo = [];
  const q = [];
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) if (indeg[i]===0) q.push(i);
  for (let i=0;i<q.length;i++) {
    const u = q[i]; topo.push(u);
    for (const [v] of adj[u]) if (--indeg[v]===0) q.push(v);
  }
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const ways = Array(n).fill(0);
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;
  dist[0] = 0; ways[0] = 1;
  for (const u of topo) {
    if (dist[u]===INF) continue;
    for (const [v,w] of adj[u]) {
      const nd = dist[u] + w;
      if (nd < dist[v]) { dist[v] = nd; ways[v] = ways[u]; }
      else if (nd === dist[v]) { ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % MOD; }
    }
  }
  return {dist, ways};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this if edges can have negative weights but the graph remains a DAG? 
- How to extend to output the K smallest distances with counts efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to combine shortest-path computation, path counting, and path reconstruction into a cohesive solution. It also evaluates handling of multiple equal-cost routes and lexicographic tie-breaking.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm for O(m log n) shortest paths
- Shortest-path DAG construction
- Topological ordering by distance values
- DP for counting distinct shortest paths
- Lexicographic path reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestStats(n, edges, s, t) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    adj[u].push([v, w]);
  }

  const dist = new Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  const pq = [[0, s]];
  dist[s] = 0;

  while (pq.length) {
    const [d, u] = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        pq.push([dist[v], v]);
      }
    }
  }

  const dag = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (let u = 0; u < n; u++) {
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w === dist[v]) {
        dag[u].push(v);
      }
    }
  }

  const ways = new Array(n).fill(0);
  ways[s] = 1;
  const order = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i)
    .sort((a, b) => dist[a] - dist[b]);

  for (const u of order) {
    for (const v of dag[u]) {
      ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % 1000000007;
    }
  }

  const path = [];
  let curr = t;
  while (curr !== null) {
    path.unshift(curr);
    const preds = dag
      .map((list, u) => list.includes(curr) ? u : -1)
      .filter(u => u >= 0 && dist[curr] === dist[u] + 
        adj[u].find(([v]) => v === curr)?.[1]);
    preds.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    curr = preds.length > 0 ? preds[0] : null;
    if (curr === s) {
      path.unshift(s);
      break;
    }
  }

  return { distance: dist[t], ways: ways[t], path };
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- Dijkstra: O(m log n)
- DAG construction: O(m)
- DP counting: O(m)
- Path reconstruction: O(n + m)
- Overall: O(m log n)

## Why This Is Asked
Path auto-complete is a common editor feature; it probes practical data-structure trade-offs beyond textbooks.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node children map
- Prefix search via traversal then DFS for completions
- Memory sharing of prefixes and separator handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.isEnd = false;
  }
}
class PathTrie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insertPath(path) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of path) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    node.isEnd = true;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, maxResults) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    const dfs = (cur, built) => {
      if (results.length >= maxResults) return;
      if (cur.isEnd) results.push(prefix + built);
      for (const [ch, child] of cur.next) dfs(child, built + ch);
    };
    dfs(node, "");
    return results;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for memory with extremely large path samples (e.g., thousands of common prefixes)?
- How would you support ranking suggestions by frequency or recency without breaking O(length) per insert?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design DP states that encode ordering constraints across variable-length tokens, a common real-world pattern in data parsing and tokenization.

## Key Concepts
- DP state design with (pos, prevLen) to compare adjacent tokens
- String comparison for equal-length numbers; no leading zeros handling
- Memoization vs bottom-up trade-offs for clarity and performance

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWays(s) {
  const n = s.length;
  const mod = 1_000_000_007;
  const memo = new Map();
  const dfs = (pos, prevLen) => {
    if (pos === n) return 1;
    const key = pos + ':' + prevLen;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    let res = 0;
    for (let nextLen = 1; nextLen <= n - pos; nextLen++) {
      if (s[pos] === '0') break;
      if (prevLen === 0 || nextLen > prevLen || (nextLen === prevLen && s.substring(pos, pos + nextLen) > s.substring(pos - prevLen, pos))) {
        res = (res + dfs(pos + nextLen, nextLen)) % mod;
      }
    }
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  };
  return dfs(0, 0);
}
```

