- How would you optimize equal-length comparisons to O(1) using precomputed LCP arrays?
- How would you adapt for non-strictly increasing sequences or allow leading zeros in some tokens?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of a practical data structure for real-world features (auto-complete) and how to extend it with per-word frequencies.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal frequency tracking
- DFS/BFS to collect candidate words from a prefix
- Sorting by frequency then lexicographic order
- Edge cases: missing prefix, zero matches, tie handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.freq = 0; // frequency for word ending here
  }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    node.freq += 1;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    const dfs = (n, path) => {
      if (n.freq > 0) results.push([path, n.freq]);
      for (const [ch, child] of n.next) dfs(child, path + ch);
    };
    dfs(node, prefix);
    results.sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
    return results.slice(0, K).map(v => v[0]);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you prune rarely-used branches to save memory?
- How would you support concurrent inserts and autocomplete in a multi-threaded environment?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests fundamental graph traversal algorithms, BFS implementation, and pathfinding skills - critical for autonomous vehicle navigation systems and route planning applications.

## Expected Answer
The candidate should implement BFS using a queue, maintain a visited set to avoid cycles, track distances from the start node, and handle edge cases such as when start equals target, disconnected graphs, or empty input. They should also explain why BFS guarantees the shortest path in unweighted graphs.

## Code Example
```python
from collections import deque

def shortest_path(graph, start, end):
    if start == end:
        return 0

    queue = deque([(start, 0)])
    visited = {start}

    while queue:
        node, distance = queue.popleft()

        for neighbor in graph.get(node, []):
            if neighbor == end:
                return distance + 1
            if neighbor not in visited:
                visited.add(neighbor)
                queue.append((neighbor, distance + 1))

    return -1
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a stable multi-key sort and apply bit-operations in a practical setting.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sort
- Two-key comparator
- Hamming weight (popcount)
- Time/space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortByWeightAndValue(arr) {
  const popcount = (n) => {
    n = n >>> 0;
    n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
    n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
    n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f;
    return (n * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
  };
  const items = arr.map(v => ({ val: v, w: popcount(v) }));
  const mergeSort = (a) => {
    if (a.length <= 1) return a.slice();
    const m = Math.floor(a.length / 2);
    const L = mergeSort(a.slice(0, m));
    const R = mergeSort(a.slice(m));
    const res = [];
    let i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < L.length && j < R.length) {
      if (L[i].w !== R[j].w) res.push(L[i].w < R[j].w ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
      else res.push(L[i].val <= R[j].val ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
    }
    while (i < L.length) res.push(L[i++]);
    while (j < R.length) res.push(R[j++]);
    return res;
  };
  const sorted = mergeSort(items);
  return sorted.map(o => o.val);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design in-place sorting algorithms, understand stability, and reason about trade-offs between time and memory in constrained environments.

## Key Concepts

- In-place merging
- Stability
- Time/space complexity
- Rotation-based merging

## Code Example

```javascript
function inPlaceMerge(arr, left, mid, right) {
  let i = left, j = mid + 1;
  while (i <= mid && j <= right) {
    if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) { i++; continue; }
    const tmp = arr[j];
    for (let k = j; k > i; k--) arr[k] = arr[k - 1];
    arr[i] = tmp;
    i++; mid++; j++;
  }
}

function inPlaceMergeSort(arr, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1) {
  if (left >= right) return;
  const mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);
  inPlaceMergeSort(arr, left, mid);
  inPlaceMergeSort(arr, mid + 1, right);
  inPlaceMerge(arr, left, mid, right);
}
```

